# Enhanced Efficiency and Stability of n‐i‐p Perovskite Solar Cells by Incorporation of Fluorinated Graphene in the Spiro‐OMeTAD Hole Transport Layer

## abstract
Spiro‐OMeTAD is one of the most used hole transport layers (HTLs) in high efficiency n‐i‐p perovskite solar cells (PSCs). However, due to the unsatisfactory conductivity of pristine Spiro‐OMeTAD, additives such as tert‐butylpyridine (tBP) and lithium bis (trifluoromethylsulfonyl)‐imide (LiTFSI) are required to improve its hole transportation. The hygroscopic nature of these additives inevitably deteriorates the device's stability. Here, it is shown that by adding fluorinated graphene (FG) into the Li‐TFSI and tBP doped Spiro‐OMeTAD, both efficiency and stability of the PSCs are significantly enhanced. Using the FG incorporated Spiro‐OMeTAD HTL, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of the PSC reaches 21.92%, which is 11.8% higher than the original device. The FG not only improves the hole mobility of Spiro‐OMeTAD but also effectively reduces the amount of lithium ions in the perovskite layer and improves the hydrophobicity of the HTL. The FG incorporating cell shows better stability, maintaining 90% of initial efficiency over a 2400 h test in ambient conditions with 25% humidity. Finally, it is further demonstrated that the valence band of FG incorporated Spiro‐OMeTAD HTL has a positive effect on PSCs with a 2D interfacial layer, achieving an impressive PCE of 23.14% and a Voc of 1.226 V.

Spiro-OMeTAD is one of the most used hole transport layers (HTLs) in high efficiency n-i-p perovskite solar cells (PSCs). However, due to the unsatisfactory conductivity of pristine Spiro-OMeTAD, additives such as tert-butylpyridine (tBP) and lithium bis (trifluoromethylsulfonyl)-imide (LiTFSI) are required to improve its hole transportation. The hygroscopic nature of these additives inevitably deteriorates the device’s stability. Here, it is shown that by adding fluorinated graphene (FG) into the Li-TFSI and tBP doped SpiroOMeTAD, both efficiency and stability of the PSCs are significantly enhanced. Using the FG incorporated Spiro-OMeTAD HTL, the power conversion efficiency (PCE) of the PSC reaches $21.92\%$ , which is $11.8\%$ higher than the original device. The FG not only improves the hole mobility of Spiro-OMeTAD but also effectively reduces the amount of lithium ions in the perovskite layer and improves the hydrophobicity of the HTL. The FG incorporating cell shows better stability, maintaining $90\%$ of initial efficiency over a $2400\textmd h$ test in ambient conditions with $25\%$ humidity. Finally, it is further demonstrated that the valence band of FG incorporated Spiro-OMeTAD HTL has a positive effect on PSCs with a 2D interfacial layer, achieving an impressive PCE of $23.14\%$ and a Voc of 1.226 V.
## 1. Introduction
As the promising commercial photovoltaic technology for third-generation solar cells, halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have attracted much attention owing to their excellent physicochemical properties, facile fabrication process, and high performance. During the past ten years, the certified power conversion efficiencies (PCEs) of PSCs have been boosted up as high as $25.5\%$ .[1–8] Generally, the conventional PSCs typically use a sandwich structure composed of an electron transport layer (ETL), a hole transport layer (HTL), a perovskite light absorbing layer, and two metal electrodes. It has been reported that the performances of PSC devices are easily affected by some environmental factors, such as ultraviolet light, temperature, and moisture.[9–17] Moreover, the material properties for each layer of the PSCs also have a profound impact on the device’s performance. For example, HTL is used to extract photo-generated holes from the perovskite layer and to prevent the recombination of charges at the metal electrode. Up to now, several hole transport materials have been applied in the device structure of conventional PSCs, including Poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6-triMethylphenyl) aMine] (PTAA),[18] Poly(3-hexylthiophene-2,5-diyl) (P3HT),[19] $^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}$ -Tetrakis (N,N-di-p-methoxyphenylamine) $^{9,9^{\prime}}$ -spirobifluorene (Spiro-OMeTAD),[20] CuSCN,[21] ${\mathrm{CuInS}}_{2}$ ,[22] etc. Among them, Spiro-OMeTAD is widely implemented in the conventional PSCs because of the matching energy level and non-reactivity characteristics between this hole transport material and the perovskite layer,[23,24] in fact the traditional SpiroOMeTAD film has certain drawbacks, such as low conductivity and poor compactness,[25–28] which are not conducive to charge transfer processes for the devices. Furthermore, due to the existence of pinholes and voids in the Spiro-OMeTAD film, it cannot effectively prevent the oxygen and moisture from penetrating into the perovskite layer, resulting in degradation of device performance. Therefore, in order to improve the conductivity of Spiro-OMeTAD, $p$ -type dopants are used to increase its conductivity, hole mobility, and filling conductivity, such as bis(trifluoromethane) sulfonimide lithium salt (Li-TFSI) and tert-butylpyridine (TBP).[29–31] However, Li-TFSI is easily deliquescent and agglomerated, thereby severely degrading the HTL and perovskite film. In addition, $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ migration in LiTFSI is also a factor that affects cell stability, causing hysteresis during forward/backward scan. Besides, because of the formation of $[\mathrm{Pb}]_{2}$ -TBP] coordination complexes, leads to inhomogeneous of the HTL films. The addition of Li-TFSi and TBP therefore accelerates the infiltration of moisture/oxygen into the perovskite layer, which seriously degrades the perovskite material and ultimately accelerates the degradation of PSCs.[32–34] Hence, in order to overcome these shortcomings, graphene functional materials were proposed as additives for Spiro-OMeTAD or others HTLs in conventional PSCs.[35–38] For example, Li et al. used graphene oxide (GO) with amphiphilic function to form a buffer layer between the perovskite and HTL. After GO modification, the corresponding average efficiency is greatly improved, from $10.0\%$ to $14.5\%$ .[39] Luo et  al. used liquid dispersions to fabricate iodine-doped reduced graphene oxide (rGO) flakes and inserted them into the undoped Spiro-OMeTAD HTL.[40] Compared with the control device $(5.72\%)$ , the device using graphene obtains a PCE of $13.01\%$ . Later, Palma et al. reported that reduced graphene oxide (rGO) was used as the only HTM to replace the traditional SpiroOMeTAD in PSC and obtained PSC with excellent stability.[41] Recently, chlorine-doped graphene has been shown to enhance the electrical conductivity of Spiro-OMeTAD films to improve cell efficiency.[42]
In previous reports, pristine graphene and hybrid graphene are widely used as an additive for HTLs in perovskite solar cells (Table S1, Supporting Information). By comparison with pristine graphene, due to the introduction of heteroatoms, by destroying the original structure of graphene, a better graphene dispersion can be obtained while maintaining the original high conductivity and hydrophobicity of graphene. Here, we propose to use F atoms for p-type doping of graphene. Compared with Cl atom-doped graphene and graphene oxide, due to the higher electronegativity of F atoms, FG has higher selectivity for electron and hole transport. This has a positive effect on the regulation of carrier transport between membranes in cells.
In this work, fluorinated graphene (FG), with low fluorine/ carbon ratio $(\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{G}\approx2\%)$ ,was added as a multifunctional additive to Spiro-OMeTAD. The FG powders were produced via hydrothermal treatment on multi-layer graphene films (see methods). The $\mathrm{~F~}$ atoms acts as p-type dopant in graphene, which leads to higher hole selectivity and mobility of the Spiro-OMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ film. The ultra-thin two-dimensional network structure of the nanosheet effectively reduce the pinholes of the HTM and increases the hydrophobicity of the HTM, which helps keep away the moisture the environment. Moreover, the defective graphene can also provide many adsorption sites for lithium ion which effectively confine the migration of lithium ion in the composite HTM layer. Consequently, the HTL device based on Spiro-OMeTAD+FG achieves a PCE of $21.92\%$ , which is higher than that of device based on pristine Spiro-OMeTAD $(19.60\%)$ . The PSC maintains $90\%$ of its initial PCE after aging for $2400\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature in $25\%$ relative humidity (RH). Finally, a champion device with PCE $(23.14\%)$ and Voc (1.226  V) is achieved by using p-butyl iodide (BAI) as 2D interfacial layer in conjuction with SpiroOMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ HTL. It is worth mentioning that in the ternary cation system, $1.226\mathrm{~V~}$ is one of the highest voltages reported so far.
## 2. Results and Discussion
Figure 1a shows the structure of PSC with $\mathrm{ITO/SnO_{2}/C s_{0.05}(F A_{0.85}M A_{0.15})_{0.95}}$ $\mathrm{Pb}$ $(\mathrm{I}_{0.85}$ $\mathrm{Br}_{0.15})_{3}/\mathrm{Spiro}\cdot$ OMeTAD/Au thin film stacks. Figure  1b shows cross-sectional views of $\mathrm{ITO}/$ $\mathrm{SnO_{2}/C s_{0.05}(F A_{0.85}~\ M A_{0.15})_{0.95}}$ $\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{0.85}\mathrm{Br}_{0.15})_{3}/$ Spiro-OMeTAD/ Au. The thickness of the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer, the $\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}(F A_{0.85}~\ M A_{0.15})_{0.95}P b(I_{0.85}~\ B r_{0.15})_{3}}$ layer and the SpiroOMeTAD layer is ${\approx}35$ , ${\approx}380$ , and ${\approx}138~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, which are close to the previously reported high-efficiency perovskite solar cells.[43]
In experiments, the Spiro-OMeTAD HTL is prepared either with or without FG additives for comparison. The morphology and structure of FG are shown in Figure S1a, Supporting Information, from which the morphology of the chemical vapor deposition (CVD) grown multilayer graphene can be clearly observed. The HR-TEM image of FG in Figure S1b, Supporting Information also shows the $0.35~\mathrm{nm}$ lattice fringe of the multilayer graphene. Comparing the Raman spectra of the pristine graphene and the FG (Figure 1c), it can be seen that the G peak of graphene has an obvious blue shift after F ion doping, which is the result of the effect of hole doping.[44] Due to the effect of F doping, the atomic arrangement disorder increases, and it can be found that the D peak intensity of the FG increases significantly compared to the pristine graphene.[45] The $I_{2\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ of the FG is ${\approx}0.5$ , which indicates that the number of graphene layers is greater than 4 layers.[46] This result is consistent with the multilayer structure observed in TEM, and the $I_{2\mathrm{D}}/\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{G}}$ ratio remains the same for graphene before and after fluorinated. The presence of carbon-bonded fluorine atoms was found in the FG sample, whose C1s peaks located at $284.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ in the XPS spectra (Figure 1d). The C 1 s peak decomposition is shown in Figure 1e. The main component at $284.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ is attributed to $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ carbon bonding, the peaks at 285.6 and $286.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ correspond to $\displaystyle\mathrm{sp}^{3}$ carbon atoms where the adjacent carbon is bonded to one or two fluorine atoms (C-CF, C-CF2).[47] The composition of the higher binding energy state of $289.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ is associated with a carbon atom bonded to a fluorine atom (C-F).[48] The F1s peak at 687.7  eV corresponds to the $\mathsf{s p}^{3}$ -hybridized chemically bonded fluoride ion,[49,50] which is covalent in character.[51,52] According to the XPS results, the $\mathrm{F/C}$ element ratio is estimated to be $2.83\%$ . It is reported that fully fluorinated graphene with $\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{C}=1:1$ ratio is an insulator. Therefore, the degree of functionalization is critical for achieving semiconducting behaviors in the fluorinated graphene. The structural diagram of graphene is shown in Figure S3, Supporting Information. Fluorinated graphene obtained by heating in a water bath, its F atoms are distributed on both sides. The calculated band structures of graphene and fluorinated graphene are shown in Figures S3e,g, Supporting Information, respectively. Fluorographene has a direct band gap of about $0.5\mathrm{eV}$ at the Γ point with a Femi level below the Dirac cone, theoretically proving the p-type behavior of the FG.[53] The DOS (density of states) of graphene and fluorinated graphene are shown in Figure $\mathrm{\DeltaS3f,h}$ , Supporting Information, respectively. The Fermi energy of the doped graphene is below the VBM, about $0.4{-}0.5\ \mathrm{eV}.^{[54]}$
To determine the exact conduction band and valence band position of the Spiro-OMeTAD+FG films, ultraviolet-visible spectroscopy (UV-vis) and ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) measurements were carried out. As shown in Figure S4, Supporting Information, the absorption wavelength range of pristine Spiro-OMeTAD and Spiro-OMeTAD+FG films are both less than $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Following Tauc-plot evaluation, as shown in Figure  1f, the extracted band gaps of the pristine Spiro-OMeTAD and Spiro-OMeTAD+FG films are 3.00 and 3.01  eV, respectively. Two lines in Figure  1g present the UPS spectra of two samples. The valence band energy level $\left(E_{\mathrm{v}}\right)$ can be obtained from the UPS spectra by the following equation, $\mathbb{W}_{\mathrm{s}}=\mathrm{hv}\mathbb{E}_{\mathrm{cutoff}}$ and $\mathrm{E_{v}}=21.21\ \mathrm{eV\cdot(E_{cutoff}\mathrm{E_{onset}})}$ . Due to the high work function of FG (about 4.70 ev),[55] the incorporated FG prominently elevates the $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{v}}$ of HTL (i.e., Spiro-OMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ ), which is $-5.14$ eV, higher than that of Spiro-OMeTAD $(-5.20\ \mathrm{eV})$ .[56] In comparison, the valence band (VB) energy level arrangement at the perovskite/HTL interface is optimized by the introduction of FG, and the HTL doped with F atoms is more conducive to hole transfer. In addition, according to the equation $\mathrm{E_{v}=E_{c}+E_{g}}$ , the conduction band energy levels $\left(\mathrm{E_{c}}\right)$ of Spiro-OMeTAD+FG films are calculated to be $\approx-2.13\ \mathrm{eV}.^{[57]}$
Hence, the $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{c}}$ of doped Spiro-OMeTAD film is slightly higher than the pristine Spiro-OMeTAD film $(-2.20\ \mathrm{eV})$ and much higher than the perovskite film $(-4.16~\mathrm{eV})$ .
The surface morphology difference between the SpiroOMeTAD device and the Spiro-OMeTAD+FG device was identified by SEM. Figure 2a shows a top view of the SpiroOMeTAD layer on top of the PSC. It clearly shows that there are many pinholes on the surface of the Spiro-OMeTAD film, which may cause severe instability problems. On the one hand, moisture and oxygen in the environment may penetrate and diffuse into the perovskite layer through these pores, resulting in the degradation of the perovskite active layer. On the other hand, the gold electrode material can also diffuse into the perovskite layer via these pinholes during the evaporation process, which would lead to higher dark-state leakage current and reduced solar cell efficiency. For comparison, the Spiro-OMeTAD+FG HTL present a compact surface without any pinholes, as shown in Figure 2b. This result suggests that the density of the Spiro-OMeTAD layer improved effectively by graphene. Figure  2c,d shows the AFM surface topography of ITO $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD and $\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ /perovskite /Spiro-OMeTAD+FG. The average surface roughness of the additive-based (FG) device is $2.03\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is slightly higher than that of the pristine Spiro-OMeTAD $\left(1.64\mathrm{nm}\right)$ . AFM analysis shows that increased surface roughness may also play a role in increasing surface hydrophobicity.[58] By comparison, it is found that there are a large number of pinholes with an average diameter of $37~\mathrm{nm}$ on the surface of pristine SpiroOMeTAD. It is suspected in the previous report that the pinholes may be caused by the hydrolysis and the migration of the Lithium salt in the HTL. The pinholes on the film surface are significantly reduced after FG is incorporated, which is consistent with the SEM topography. The HTL surface potential distribution of the two devices is characterized by KPFM under light. The corresponding potential distributions of the undoped FG and doped HTL films are shown in Figure $^{2\mathrm{d},\mathrm{f},}$ respectively. Compared with the control device, the film containing FG has higher surface potential, which indicates that doped graphene is more favorable for hole transfer at the perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ interface. Figure  2g,h shows the water contact angle of the films. The contact angle of the device based on Spiro-OMeTAD+FG film and pristine SpiroOMeTAD are about $63^{\circ}$ and $18^{\circ}$ . The lager angle manifests that the introduction of graphene can effectively isolate $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in the air. Furthermore, the influence of the introduction of FG on the conductivity of HTL was studied. Considering the high charge transport properties of carbon materials, it is expected that the FG would improve the electrical conductivity of SpiroOMeTAD.[59,60] Figure  2i shows the $J{-}V$ characteristic HTL of two kinds of resistors sandwiched between ITO and Au. $\sigma$ can be obtained using the following formula: $\rho=\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{R}{\cdot}\mathrm{S}$ , where L is the thickness of the HTL, R is the resistance, and S is the common area between ITO and Au. $1/\mathrm{R}{\cdot}\mathrm{S}$ can be obtained from the slope after curve fitting in Figure 2i. The results show that the $\sigma$ of Spiro-OMeTAD is $5.90\times10^{-4}\mathrm{{S}\thinspace{c m}^{-1}}$ , which is basically consistent with the previous report,[61] and the $\sigma$ of SpiroOMeTAD+FG is $3.64\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{S\cm^{-1}}$ , which is ${\approx}6$ times higher than that of the pristine Spiro-OMeTAD.
To achieve the best performance of PSC with FG doped Spiro-OMeTAD layer (Spiro-OMeTAD+FG), it is necessary to optimize the concentration of FG in Spiro-OMeTAD HTL. Figure S5, Supporting Information shows the typical histogram and distribution of PCE with various concentrations (inset). Table S2, Supporting Information lists the performance parameters of PSCs with different concentrations of FG. It can be found that the optimal FG concentration in the precursor is $0.025\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ . The corresponding current density versus voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of devices with different concentrations are shown in Figure S6, Supporting Information. Figure 3a shows the $J{-}V$ curves of the champion PSCs based on SpiroOMeTAD with and without FG under AM 1.5G illumination. The performance results of the champion device are summarized in Table S3, Supporting Information. The device with Spiro-OMeTAD+FG shows an open circuit voltage $(V_{O C})$ of $1.170~\mathrm{V},$ a short circuit current density $(J_{S C})$ of $23.99\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and a fill factor $(F F)$ of $78.11\%$ , giving a $P C E$ of $21.92\%$ during reverse scanning (RS). Compared with the device using conventional Spiro-OMeTAD film, the PSC of the device with SpiroOMeTAD+FG has increased more than $10\%$ . It is also worth to note that little hysteresis is found in the $J{\cdot}V$ curve of the SpiroOMeTAD+FG device under backward and forward scanning, resulting in a high PCE of $21.24\%$ under forward scanning (FS) $\mathrm{'}V_{O C}=1.165\mathrm{~V}_{:}$ , $J_{S C}=23.72~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $\mathrm{FF}=76.77\%)$ . In contrast, a large PCE difference under RS $(19.60\%)$ and under FS $(17.93\%)$ is observed in the device with conventional SpiroOMeTAD HTL. The integrated photocurrent values from the incident  photon-to-electron  conversion  efficiency (IPCE) in Figure  3b, $20.83\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for the Spiro-OMeTAD device and $22.10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for the Spiro-OMeTAD+FG device, are consistent with the measured $J_{S C}$ value. It is worth mentioning that the performance of the device using HTL with pristine graphene is not as good as the device using HTL with FG. As shown in Figure S7, Supporting Information, compared with the control device, the performance of the device is not improved. By further counting 300 devices (Figure S8, Supporting Information), it was found that the yielding rate of the devices using pristine graphene as additive in HTL is much lower than that of making devices with FG. This may be due to the fact that the pure graphene cannot be effectively dispersed in chlorobenzene solvent. In order to explore the operating state of the PSC, the steady-state output at the maximum power point (MPP) was shown in Figure 3d. The initial PCE remained $95\%$ for the Spiro-OMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ under continuous MPP for $300~\mathrm{s}$ , while the control device dropped to $88\%$ . The long-term stability of unpackaged devices can be tested by storing them at room temperature in an environment with a relative humidity (RH) of approximately $25\%$ . As shown in Figure 3e, during the 2400 h monitoring period, the PCE of the Spiro-OMeTAD+FG based device lost $\approx10\%$ , while the Spiro-OMeTAD based device lost $\approx20\%$ of the initial efficiency. To speed up the degradation process, the devices were further stored at a condition with a relative humidity (RH) of $\approx45\%$ . After $4600\mathrm{~h~}$ , the Spiro-OMeTAD device fails and only maintains $56\%$ of the initial efficiency, while the Spiro-OMeTAD $^+$ FG-based still exhibits more than $80\%$ of the original efficiency. Therefore, the device based on Spiro-OMeTAD $^+$ FG material shows enhanced stability, which may be mainly originated from the better hydrophobicity and less pinholes of the Spiro-OMeTAD+FG HTL. The $J{-}V$ curves of the initial and aged devices are shown in Figure 3f. The statistical distribution of 20 cells of each type of device in Figure 3d clearly shows that the average PCE of solar cells was significantly increased from $18.98\%$ to $21.09\%$ when Spiro-OMeTAD was replaced with Spiro-OMeTAD $+$ FG. The corresponding $V_{O C},$ FF, and $J_{S C}$ statistical graphs of these devices are displayed in Figure S9, Supporting Information, which confirms that the average $V_{O C},$ , FF or Jsc of the PCS devices has been significantly improved with Spiro-OMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ HTL.
Depth profiles of several key elements in PSCs determined by time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry (TOF-SIMS) are shown in Figure  3g. TOF-SIMS can detect elemental and molecular species with sensitivity in parts per million or higher, with a dynamic range of four orders of magnitude. Two types of PSCs were analyzed. From the TOF-SIMS depth profiles of different HTL-based devices (Figure  3h,i), it is clear that in both PSCs, the $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ion concentration does not obey a simple Fickian diffusion profile, and the concentration in the $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ layer higher than the concentration in the perovskite layer. This indicates that $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ions have a lower affinity for perovskites than $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . The same trend of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ion distribution has also been reported in previous literature reports.[62,63] It is clear from the TOF-SIMS depth profiles of different HTL-based devices (Figure 3h,i) that a large amount of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ migrates from the SpiroOMeTAD layer to the perovskite layer. In contrast, the FG-based Spiro-OMeTAD device has a nearly 1.6-fold reduction in free $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ in the perovskite layer, indicating that the $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ migration from the Spiro-OMeTAD layer to the perovskite layer is effectively suppressed. In the pristine Spiro-OMeTAD-based device, since the amorphous Spiro-OMeTAD film is prone to aggregation and crystallization, moisture can easily penetrate into the perovskite layer through the intrusion path in the SpiroOMeTAD layer, resulting in the degradation of the perovskite. This means that the incorporation of graphene can inhibit the migration of ${\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ from the Spiro-OMeTAD layer to the perovskite layer, thereby reducing the path for moisture and oxygen to enter the perovskite layer.[64] This is one of the main reasons for improved cellular stability and reduced current hysteresis in Spiro-OMeTAD+FG-based PSCs.
Steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved photo­luminescence (TRPL) were performed to reveal the charge transfer dynamics at the perovskite film and Spiro-OMeTAD interface. Figure 4a compares the steady-state PL spectrum of the ITO/perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD thin film and the $\mathrm{ITO}/$ perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ thin film, with excitation laser launched from the glass side. The PL intensity of perovskite with Spiro-OMeTAD+FG is greatly reduced, which indicates that the Spiro-OMeTAD+FG layer can extract charge carriers more effectively. This result explains the reason why higher FF and smaller hysteresis were achieved in the PSC with SpiroOMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ HTL in Figure 3a.[65] The TRPL curve (Figure 4b) is fitted to the double exponential decay equation of ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}}({\boldsymbol{\mathrm{t}}})={\boldsymbol{\mathrm{I}}}_{0}+$ $\mathrm{A_{1}e x p}(-\mathrm{t}/\tau_{1})+\mathrm{A_{2}e x p}(-\mathrm{t}/\tau_{2})$ , where $\tau_{1}$ and $\tau_{2}$ are the fast and slow decay lifetimes. $\mathbf{A}_{1}$ and $\mathbf{A}_{2}$ are the relative amplitudes respectively. The detailed fitting data is shown in Table S5, Supporting Information. The fast decay time constant $\tau_{1}$ is derived from the rapid transfer and extraction of holes at the perovskite/HTL interface, and the slow decay time constant $\tau_{2}$ depends on the recombination of photogenerated carriers in the perovskite layer. The $\tau_{1}$ decreases from 85.86  ns of the perovskite/SpiroOMeTAD to 48.85  ns of the perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ , indicating that the more efficient transfer rate at the perovskite/ Spiro-OMeTAD+FG interface. In addition, the $\tau_{2}$ of the perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ film is $10.04\mathrm{ns}$ , which is higher than that of the perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD $(7.05~\mathrm{ns})$ . These results suggest that Spiro-OMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ has a better ability to extract charge carriers from perovskite and that the charge recombination of perovskite films with Spiro-OMeTAD+FG is reduced.[66] The improvement of Voc indicates that radiative recombination was improved due to the reduction of defect density.[67]
To address the improved performance, carrier dynamics were further investigated. Transient photocurrent (TPC) tests related to carrier recombination and transient photovoltage (TPV) tests related to carrier transport were performed, respectively.[15] Figure 4c shows TPC decay under short-circuit conditions to understand the effect of fluorinated graphene on the charge transfer properties in solar cells. The photocurrent decay lifetimes were calculated using a single exponential decay fit as 1.99 and $3.90~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ , respectively, for devices without and with fluorinated graphene films. The delay time of the fluorinated graphene-based device is slightly shorter than that of the device without fluorinated graphene. This indicates that the transfer of holes from the perovskite to the treated Spiro-OMeTAD is more efficient than the transfer to the pristine Spiro-OMeTAD. Together with its higher conductivity, the treated SpiroOMeTAD enables more efficient charge transport.[68] TPV decay measurements indicate the recombination velocity of injected electrons, from which charge carrier lifetimes can be extracted. Rapid photovoltage decay indicates rapid recombination. As shown in Figure  4d, the charge recombination lifetime $(\tau)$ of the devices containing fluorinated graphene was found to be slightly longer than that of the devices without fluorinated graphene (i.e., $8.77~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ based on fluorinated graphene vs $4.90~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ based on fluorinated graphene-free PSCs), calculated using a single exponential decay fit to the experimental data. The lower charge carrier recombination velocity in the fluorinated graphene device compared to the reference further supports the improved carrier extraction from the perovskite layer to the Spiro-OMeTAD HTL.[69,70]
The space charge limited current (SCLC) model is used to further investigate the charge transport characteristics of perovskite films/HTL, by using a pure hole device of $\mathrm{ITO}/$ PEDOT:PSS/perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD/Ag. The dark state I-V curves of different HTLs was shown in Figure  4e. The hole mobility of different HTL devices can be obtained according to the Mott–Gurney square law:[71]
$$
J={\frac{9}{8}}\varepsilon_{\mathrm{0}}\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}\mu_{\mathrm{h}}{\frac{\left(V_{\mathrm{a}}-V_{\mathrm{bi}}\right)^{2}}{L^{3}}}
$$
where $J$ is the current density, $\varepsilon_{0}$ is the dielectric constant of vacuum, $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}$ is the relative dielectric constant of the perovskite active layer, $\upmu_{\mathrm{h}}$ is the hole mobility, and $L$ is the thickness of the perovskite active layer, $V_{\mathrm{a}}$ is the applied voltage, and $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ is the built-in field caused by the difference in work function between Ag and ITO. Calculation results show that the hole mobility of HTL/perovskite based on Spiro-OMeTAD+FG devices has greatly increased from $5.20\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ (pristine SpiroOMeTAD) to $3.21\times10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ (Spiro-OMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ ). As an efficient HTL, an important role of Spiro-OMeTAD is to transfer holes to the cell anode with minimal loss, which requires hole transport materials with efficient hole transfer capability. According to the IV characteristic in Figure 4e, the hole trap-state density $(\mathrm{N_{trap}})$ can be estimated from the trap-filled limit voltage $(\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{TFL}})$ as follows: $\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{trap}}{=}2\varepsilon_{0}\varepsilon\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{TFL}}/\mathrm{eL}^{2}$ , where $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{TFL}}$ is the starting voltage of the trap-filled limit area, e is an electronic charge. The calculation results are summarized in Table S6, Supporting Information. The results show that the defect density at the interface between Spiro-OMeTAD and perovskite doped with FG is greatly reduced, which is due to the defects brought about by the intrusion of the moisture into the perovskite during the oxidation process.[72] The Spiro-OMeTAD+FG has enabled higher hole mobility and reduced trap states, thereby helps improve device photovoltaic performance. Figure  4f shows the dark state $I{-}V$ curves obtained for a pure hole device of glass/ $\mathrm{Au/HTL}/\mathrm{Au},^{[73]}$ where the ordinate is the dark current density of the pure hole diode and the abscissa is the applied voltage. The hole mobility $(\upmu)$ of the HTL can also be calculated by using the Mott–Gurney square law. The pristine Spiro-OMeTAD exhibits an electron mobility of $2.01\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , which is comparable to previously reported values.[74,75] At the same time, we found that the fluorinated graphene composite HTL exhibits higher hole mobility (e.g, the $\upmu$ of Spiro-OMeTAD+FG is $2.52\times10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}\mathrm{V}^{-1}\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ . We propose that fluorinated graphene may lead to a more compact packing of Spiro-OMeTAD film, which may be responsible for the enhanced hole mobility. The Spiro-OMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ has enabled higher hole mobility, thereby helping improve device photovoltaic performance.
The above conclusions were further confirmed by Mott– Schottky (M–S) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy analysis. The M–S measurement is used to reveal the charge accumulation/extraction conditions of HTL/perovskite. As shown in Figure S10, Supporting Information, according to the equation:[76] $\mathbf{C}^{-2}=2$ $(\mathrm{V_{bi}{\cdot}V)/(A^{2}q\varepsilon\varepsilon_{0}N_{d})}$ , the charge density of devices $\mathrm{(N_{d})}$ is extracted as $1.02\times10^{18}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ for perovskite/ Spiro-OMeTAD and $7.27\ \times\ 10^{17}\ \mathrm{~cm}^{-3}$ for perovskite/SpiroOMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ , respectively. There is less charge accumulation at the perovskite/Spiro-OMeTAD $+\mathrm{FG}$ interface, which should be attributed to the enhanced charge extraction efficiency enabled by incorporation of FG. EIS is performed to study the recombination kinetics and charge transport at the HTL/perovskite interface.[77] The test was carried out under simulated AM $1.5\mathrm{~G~}$ light illumination. The equivalent circuit model is shown in the inset in Figure S11, Supporting Information, including series resistance $\left(R_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ , charge transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{trans}})$ , and charge composite resistance $(R_{\mathrm{rec}})$ . By fitting the spectrum (Table S7, Supporting Information), the $R_{\mathrm{trans}}$ values for with and without FG are 1024 and $1300~\Omega$ . The charge transfer resistance of the device with Spiro-OMeTAD+FG is smaller, indicating that the charge extraction process at the electrode is more effective. We attribute this to the enhanced conductivity of the Spiro-OMeTAD film caused by the doping of FG. Meanwhile, the $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ value of the device with FG additives is higher (6847 Ω) than the device without FG (4490 Ω).[78] This suggests that in the devices obtained with FG have a slower speed of charge carriers to recombination. The reduced charge transfer resistance and enhanced charge composite resistance of FGtreated devices are qualitatively related to the observed improvements in Voc, Jsc, FF, and PCE.
Having confirmed that the incorporation of FG is One effective strategy to passivate the perovskite film is to introduce a 2D perovskite layer, such as Butylammonium iodide (BAI), on the surface of a 3D perovskite film.[17,79] With $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{v}}$ (−5.56 ev) and $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{c}}$ $(-3.92\ \mathrm{ev})$ , the BAI has the ideal energy level to bridge the 3D perovskite materials and the Spiro-OMeTAD HTL.[80] The energy band diagram in Figure 5b predicts the BAI provides a potential gradient to facilitate hole transfer between perovskite and Spiro-OMeTAD HTL. On this basis, in order to reveal the full potential of our Spiro-OMeTAD+FG HTL, BAI was spin-coated onto the perovskite film to obtain a 2D–3D PSC (Figure 5a). Surprisingly, the 2D–3D PSC with FG achieved a remarkable PCE of $23.14\%$ , with Voc of 1.226  V, Jsc of $24.37~\mathrm{mA~cm^{-2}}$ , and FF of $77.43\%$ (Figure 5b). The integrated current of the 3D/2D perovskite solar cell is $22.71\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (Figure S12, Supporting Information). According to the statistics, the voltage based on the best work of the triple-cation  mixed-halide perovskite (Table 1), the reported $1.226\mathrm{~V~}$ in this work is one of the highest $V_{O C}$ reported so far.
## 3. Conclusion
In this article, we prove that the incorporation of fluorinated graphene into Spiro-OMeTAD HTL is an effective way to enhance both the power conversion efficiency and the stability of n-i-p perovskite solar cells. It is worth noting that choosing fluorinated graphene with suitable functionalization degree is the key to the success of efficient and stable devices. The SpiroOMeTAD with FG provides enhanced HTL conductivity and lower valance band for effective hole extraction. In addition, the time-resolved photoluminescence results and transient photocurrent measurements confirms that the trap density at the interface between the perovskite and HTL decreases with addition of FG, suppressing the recombination of photogenerated carriers at the interface. Compared to the control device, the maximum PCE value of the optimized device is increased by $11.8\%$ with superior stability. The FG incorporated device maintains $90\%$ of its initial PCE over $2400\mathrm{~h~}$ test under an ambient atmosphere with relative humidity (RH) of $25\%$ , and subsequently reaching $80\%$ of its initial PCE over another $2200\mathrm{h}$ test under an elevated humidity (RH) of $45\%$ . Moreover, the proposed FG incorporated Spiro-OMeTAD HTL is ideal for highly efficient perovskite solar cell with 2D top interfacial perovskite layer. The 3D/2D PSC achieved an impressive PCE above $23\%$ with a record high Voc of $1.226\mathrm{~V~}$ among the triplecation-based perovskite devices. This work provides a reliable and economical approach for fabricating high-performance n-i-p perovskite solar cells with enhanced long-term stability.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials and Precursor Preparation: Butylammonium Iodide (BAI, $99.5\%\$ , Methylammonium Iodide (MAI, $99.5\%\AA$ , Formamidinium iodide (FAI, $99.9\%$ , Lead Iodide $(\mathsf{P b l}_{2},\ 99.99\%)$ , Cesium Iodide (CsI, $99.9\%$ , Lead Bromide $(\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ , $99.9\%$ ), 2, $2^{\prime\prime}$ , 7, $7^{\prime\prime}$ -Tetrakis [N, N-di (4-methoxyphenyl) amino]-9, $9^{\prime\prime}$ -Spirobifluorene (Spiro-OMeTAD, $99.8\%$ , 4-tert-butylpyridine (4-TBP, $96\%$ ), bis (trifluoromethylsu- lfonyl)- imide lithium salt (Li-TFSI). The above materials were purchased from Xi’an Polymer Optical Technology Co., Ltd. dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO, $99\%$ ), N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99\%$ ), chlorobenzene (CB, $99.5\%)$ , Isopropanol (IPA, $99\%$ ) were purchased from Sigma– Aldrich. Tin oxide aqueous solution $(5n O_{2};75\%$ mass in $H_{2}O$ colloidal dispersion with a few organic solvents) was purchased from Alfa Aesar.
Graphene is obtained by CVD. The $10\ \mathsf{c m}\times\mathsf{l}0\ \mathsf{c r}$ m nickel foil was ultrasonically cleaned with deionized water and ethanol for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Blow dry with nitrogen and put it into a CVD tube furnace. In an atmosphere of $700\ s c c m\ A r$ and $50\mathsf{s c c m}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ , the temperature is increased to 1050 degrees Celsius at a heating rate of 20 degrees $\mathsf{C}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ , and the temperature is maintained at 100 sccm methane for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . After it is naturally cooled to room temperature, the nickel foil is taken out and placed in a $\mathrm{~7~mol~L^{-1}~}$ $\mathsf{F e C l}_{3}\mathrm{+}0.5\ m o l/L$ HCl solution for etching for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After the etching is complete, rinse with deionized water three times and transfer to $0.4\ m o l\ L^{-1}$ HF solution. After $12\mathrm{~h~}$ of water heating at $80~^{\circ}C.$ take it out and wash with deionized water three times. Fully dry the sample in a vacuum drying oven.[85]
The $15\mathrm{\:wt\%}$ tin dioxide nano-dispersed solution was diluted with DIW to $10\mathrm{\ut\%}$ to prepare an ETL solution. $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}~385.6~\mathsf{m g}$ , $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}\ 85.6\ \mathsf{m g}$ FAI $143.9~\mathrm{mg}$ , MAI $23.4~\mathrm{mg}$ , and $73.5~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{C s l}$ were dissolved in $7m L$ of a mixture of DMSO and DMF (volume ratio 1:4) and stirred for $^{7\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain the perovskite precursor solution. Dissolve $72.3~\mathsf{m g}$ SpiroOMeTAD in $1m L$ chlorobenzene, stir for $30~\mathsf{m i n}$ , then add $77.5~\upmu\up L$ Li-TFSI ( $520~{\mathsf{m g}}$ Li-TFSI dissolved in $1m L$ acetonitrile) and $28.5~\upmu\upiota$ 4-TBP, the Spiro-OMeTAD solution could be obtained by stirring at room temperature for $6\textup{h}$ . After that, the graphene flakes were dispersed in chlorobenzene of different concentrations $(0.025~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}})$ , the mixture was sonicated for $20~\mathrm{min}$ , until a uniform dispersion is obtained. The FG not only maintains the original high work function and high conductivity of graphene, but also increases the dispersibility of graphene in the chlorobenzene solution. The suspension was then centrifuged at 3000 rpm for 5 min to remove large aggregates. and then magnetically stirred for 1 hour to obtain a chlorobenzene solution containing graphene.[86] Then repeat the above steps to obtain a Spiro-OMeTAD solution containing FG. Dissolve $5~\mathrm{mg}$ BAI in $7m L$ of IPA to obtain the perovskite passivation layer solution.
Solar cells Fabrication: Use 30 min of deionized water, $30~\mathsf{m i n}$ of acetone, and $30\mathrm{\min}$ of isopropanol followed by ultrasonic cleaning to clean the ITO $_{(\approx8\mathrm{~}\Omega}$ sheet resistance, ${\approx}94\%$ light transmittance) substrate. The cleaned substrate is treated with UV-O for 30 min. $10\mathrm{\Omega}\mathrm{wt\%}$ tin dioxide was spin-coated on the substrate after cooling to room temperature at 6500  rpm and annealed at $\mathsf{l}50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $5\mathrm{\min}$ . The $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ - coated substrate was then treated with UV-O for $\rceil\boldsymbol{\mathsf{h}}$ , and then the sample was quickly transferred to a glove box under a nitrogen atmosphere. The perovskite precursor solution was spin-coated on ${\mathsf{I T O}}/{\mathsf{S n O}}_{2}$ at a low speed of $5\mathrm{~s~}7000\mathrm{~r~}$ and then at a high speed of $30\mathrm{~s~}6000\mathrm{~r.}$ At $30\mathrm{~s~}$ , quickly add $200~\upmu\upiota$ of chlorobenzene as anti-solvent. The spin-coated film was annealed at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $15\mathrm{\min}$ . After the sample dropped to room temperature, spin-coated the BAI solution at a speed of $6000~\mathfrak{r}$ for $25\mathrm{~s~}$ and annealed at $\mathsf{l o o}^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $10\min$ . In order to prepare the HTL, the preprepared Spiro-OMeTAD (Spiro-OMeTAD $+{\mathsf{F}}{\mathsf{G}}$ ) precursor was spincoated onto the calcium on titanium ore film (5000 rpm, 15 s). The gold electrode of about $700\:\mathsf{n m}$ was deposited on the HTL by vacuum thermal evaporation. The preparation process is the same for all PSCs, and the effective area is $0.04~\mathsf{c m}^{2}$ .
Characterization and Measurements: The light source used in the light test involved in the article is a 450 watt xenon lamp (oriel solar simulator). The $J{-}V$ curve of PSC is measured under a light source calibrated to $\mathsf{l}00\ \mathsf{M}\mathsf{W}\ \mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ with a silicon reference cell at room temperature. The solar cell QE/IPCE (quantum efficiency) measurement system (Solar Cell Scan 100) is used to analyze the incident photon-electron conversion efficiency (IPCE). TPV decay and rise and TPC decay measurements were performed using a modular test bench (Arkeo, Cicci research s.r.l.) with white light bias generated by a diode array to simulate operating conditions of 0.5 solar bias light. A pulsed red dye laser pumped by a nitrogen laser was used as the perturbation source with a pulse width of 4  ns and a repetition frequency of ${\mathsf{10}}~{\mathsf{H z}}$ . Open circuit voltage and short circuit current were measured on 1 MΩ and $50~\Omega$ resistors and recorded on a digital oscilloscope (Tektronix DPO 4104B). The Chi660e electrochemical measurement workstation was used to complete the measurement of SCLC, Mot-Schottky (M–S), and conductivity under dark and standard sunlight conditions. The tested devices were measured after oxidizing Spiro-OMeTAD for 24 hours in an oxygen atmosphere at $25\%$ relative humidity. The UV absorption spectrum of the film was measured in the wavelength range of 200–1000 nm by an ultraviolet-vis spectrophotometer (UV-2450, Japan). The UPS spectra were collected on the X-ray Photoelectron Spectrometer (Thermo Fisher, ESCALAB 250Xi, UK). The SEM image was obtained by a scanning electron microscope (ZEISS SUPRA 55, Germany) at an accelerating voltage of $5\mathsf{\ k V.}$ Bruker Atomic Force Microscope (AFM) (MDTC-EQ-M16-01) was used for Kelvin Probe Force Microscope (KPFM) measurement. The TEM image was taken by FEI spirit T12 (JEM-3200FS, Japan). The film thickness measurements required for calculation were all measured with a profilometer (DEKTAK XT, Germany). The water contact angle of the film was measured using a contact angle measuring instrument (MDTC-EQ-M07-01, KRUSS DSA30). A TOF-SIMS S (ION-TOF-SIMS V) was used to depth-profile the perovskite materials and completed devices. Analysis was completed using a 3-lens 30-kV Bi-Mn primary ion gun, the $\mathsf{B i}^{+}$ primary-ion beam (operated in bunched mode; 10-ns pulse width, analysis current of $1.0~\mathsf{p A})$ was scanned over a $99\times99~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}^{2}$ area. Depth profiling was accomplished with a 1-kV oxygen-ion sputter beam ( $70.8~\mathsf{n A}$ sputter current) raster of $250\times250~\upmu\mathrm{m}^{2}$ area.
